1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
Welcome to Respiratory Tract Pathology.

2
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:03,820
The first section is the nasopharynx.

3
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:06,420
The first disorder to discuss is rhinitis.

4
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:09,680
Rhinitis basically is inflammation of the nasomucosa.

5
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:14,440
You know that the nasomucosa is lined by respiratory-type epithelium.

6
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:16,800
Adenovirus is the most common cause.

7
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:20,100
This classically presents with sneezing, congestion, and runny nose,

8
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:21,580
so basically your common cold.

9
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,180
Again, adenovirus being the most common cause.

10
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:27,700
It is also possible to get an allergic rhinitis,

11
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,620
and this would be an inflammation of the nasomucosa

12
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:33,580
due to a type 1 hypersensitivity reaction.

13
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:37,300
And, of course, with a type 1 hypersensitivity reaction,

14
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:39,820
we're going to be looking for an antigen,

15
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:41,760
and in this particular case you'll find one.

16
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:46,560
For example, pollen would be something that could elicit an allergic rhinitis.

17
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:48,760
Because this is an allergic-type reaction,

18
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:51,140
it's going to be inflammation with eosinophils,

19
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:57,640
and it's going to be associated with other types of atopic disorders

20
00:00:57,640 --> 00:00:59,780
such as asthma and eczema.

21
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:02,920
Now, one of the consequences of rhinitis,

22
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,880
and this is probably the most high yield in this particular discussion,

23
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:07,780
is that you can get nasal polyps.

24
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,060
So patients who have repeated bouts of rhinitis,

25
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:12,420
they develop nasal polyps.

26
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:18,300
And a polyp, again, is just a protrusion of edematous-inflamed nasal mucosa.

27
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:20,440
All right, now here comes the high yield.

28
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:24,600
The first high yield is that if you have a child with nasal polyps,

29
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:26,620
you really want to think cystic fibrosis.

30
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:27,880
Again, very high yield.

31
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:29,980
If you have a child with nasal polyps,

32
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,240
you want to test that child for cystic fibrosis,

33
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,720
and that becomes particularly high yield for examination purposes.

34
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:40,080
The second high yield here is that there are a subgroup of adults

35
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:42,320
who also develop nasal polyps

36
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:45,320
because they have something called aspirin-intolerant asthma.

37
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:51,780
This is a triad of asthma, aspirin-induced bronchospasm, and nasal polyps.

38
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:54,360
And it's seen in 10% of asthmatic adults.

39
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:57,660
So remember that patients with aspirin-intolerant asthma

40
00:01:57,660 --> 00:01:59,720
can also develop nasal polyps.

41
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:01,720
So those are the two high yields on this slide.

42
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:05,000
The next disorder is angiofibroma.

43
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:07,560
Angio means blood vessel, fibro means fibrous tissue,

44
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:08,940
and of course, oma means mass.

45
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:11,860
So this is a benign tumor of the nasal mucosa

46
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:14,500
comprised of blood vessels and fibrous tissue.

47
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:16,120
Again, the name gives it away.

48
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:18,940
Now, one important thing, and very high yield,

49
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,540
it's only seen in adolescent males.

50
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:22,960
That's the classic demographic.

51
00:02:23,220 --> 00:02:25,500
It is very, very, very rare in a female.

52
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:29,280
So for your purposes, it's basically a tumor of adolescent males.

53
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:32,040
And these can bleed because of the large blood vessels,

54
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:34,960
and so they can present with profuse epistaxis,

55
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:38,600
and that's the classic presentation, especially on examinations.

56
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,320
Nasopharyngeal carcinoma

57
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:44,800
is a malignant tumor of nasopharyngeal epithelium.

58
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:47,920
Of course, it's arising from the nasopharynx,

59
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:49,160
hence it's nasopharyngeal,

60
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:52,480
and it is epithelium, hence the word carcinoma.

61
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:54,320
So the name really tells you a lot there.

62
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:57,860
Now, basically, this is an EBV-driven tumor.

63
00:02:58,040 --> 00:02:59,480
That's particularly high yield.

64
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:01,700
Also high yield to know is that

65
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:03,660
it usually arises in two demographics,

66
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:07,460
African children and Chinese young adults.

67
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:09,640
The classic presentation is that

68
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:12,280
they give you, especially on examinations,

69
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:16,000
a Chinese adult who has enlarged cervical lymph nodes

70
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,380
because this tumor usually can spread

71
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:19,920
to the cervical lymph nodes very quickly,

72
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:21,180
and it classically presents

73
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:23,220
with involvement of the cervical lymph nodes.

74
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:24,820
A biopsy is then performed,

75
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,560
and the biopsy shows this classic finding,

76
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:30,360
which is pleomorphic epithelial cells

77
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:31,980
in a background of lymphocytes.

78
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:34,600
Now, these epithelial cells are scattered

79
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:36,300
within the background of lymphocytes,

80
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:38,140
and so often you have to prove

81
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:39,920
that they're actually epithelial,

82
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:43,320
and so keratin is often performed in these

83
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:46,360
to ensure that there is a carcinoma.

84
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:47,780
And so it's very high yield

85
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:49,900
for purposes of board examination

86
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:52,760
to know that this is the histology,

87
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:54,060
and what they like to tell you

88
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:55,320
is that there's a Chinese male

89
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:57,080
he presents with cervical adenopathy,

90
00:03:57,200 --> 00:03:58,080
a biopsy is performed,

91
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:00,540
and that the pathology report says

92
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:03,520
that there is pleomorphic keratin-positive cells.

93
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:05,720
Of course, keratin-positive means

94
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:07,020
that these are epithelial,

95
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:09,640
because keratin is the intermediate filament

96
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:10,780
in epithelial cells,

97
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:13,040
and so once you have keratin-positive cells,

98
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:14,860
you know that you're dealing with a carcinoma.

99
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:17,240
And so nasopharyngeal carcinoma

100
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:19,760
is a classic entity you think about

101
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,420
with this particular story.

102
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:24,140
As far as the larynx is concerned,

103
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:26,880
the first disorder is acute epiglottitis.

104
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:29,200
Acute epiglottitis refers to

105
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:31,380
inflammation of the epiglottis.

106
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:35,720
The most common cause is H-flu, type B,

107
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:37,700
and this is the most common cause

108
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:40,980
both in immunized and in non-immunized children.

109
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:43,640
So H-flu, type B, is the most common cause

110
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:46,140
in both immunized and non-immunized children.

111
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:47,960
Now you're well aware of the fact

112
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:51,060
that the epiglottis sits at the back of the throat

113
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:52,260
behind the tongue,

114
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:53,740
and so you get inflammation,

115
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,580
massive inflammation of the earway,

116
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:56,540
and so you can see

117
00:04:56,540 --> 00:04:59,520
what an inflamed epiglottis might look like,

118
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:01,320
and the patients will present

119
00:05:01,320 --> 00:05:03,700
with high fever, sore throat,

120
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:05,460
because of the inflammation in that region,

121
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:08,240
and then they get drooling with dysphagia

122
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:10,620
because of the fact that the epiglottis is swollen,

123
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:12,180
and hence the airway is compromised.

124
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:14,240
They have a muffled voice

125
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:15,360
because they're speaking

126
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:17,360
with something in the back of their throat,

127
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:19,700
and they have inspiratory stridor

128
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:21,980
because of, again, the compromise to the airway.

129
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:24,100
And this is really the key thing

130
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:24,960
that you have to worry about,

131
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:26,020
which is that there's a risk

132
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:27,120
of acute airway obstruction.

133
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:29,300
So this disorder is a medical emergency

134
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:30,900
because you need to protect the airway

135
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:32,380
in these patients.

136
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:36,560
Next disorder, laryngotracheobronchitis.

137
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:38,420
So, again, the name tells you

138
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:39,380
that you're getting inflammation

139
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:40,460
of the upper airway.

140
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:43,720
Most common cause is parainfluenza virus,

141
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:45,100
and these are the patients

142
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:46,120
that classically present

143
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:47,800
with this hoarse barking cough

144
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:49,760
and then an inspiratory stridor.

145
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:52,940
So that's laryngotracheobronchitis.

146
00:05:52,940 --> 00:05:56,520
Vocal cord nodules are nodules

147
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:57,840
that arise on the vocal cord,

148
00:05:58,140 --> 00:06:00,080
and it's classically due to excessive use.

149
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:01,920
So because it's due to excessive use,

150
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:03,120
it's often bilateral.

151
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:04,700
So you'll see one next to the other.

152
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:06,820
These are composed of degenerative

153
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:08,160
myxoid connective tissue,

154
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:10,000
and it's going to present with hoarseness

155
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,340
because of the disruption to the vocal cord.

156
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:14,560
Now, this is a wear and tear issue,

157
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:17,360
and so it occurs because the patients

158
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:18,860
have had excessive use,

159
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:21,020
and, of course, it will resolve with rest.

160
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:22,240
Here's what it looks like.

161
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:24,140
You can see one vocal cord nodule here,

162
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:25,360
another one here,

163
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:26,900
and these are bilateral

164
00:06:26,900 --> 00:06:29,160
because of the fact that it's due to

165
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:32,360
a wear and tear or use-related problem.

166
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:33,760
Now, here's what you see

167
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:35,200
if you were to do the histology,

168
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:36,600
and all I'm trying to highlight here

169
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:39,360
is that you get myxoid degeneration

170
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:40,900
of the connective tissue,

171
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:43,240
which leads to the formation

172
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:44,760
of this vocal cord nodule.

173
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:47,080
Laryngeal papilloma is actually

174
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:48,300
a tumor of the vocal cord.

175
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:50,420
You get a papillary or finger-like projection

176
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:51,340
off the vocal cord,

177
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:52,620
and it's benign.

178
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:55,260
Of course, it's very important to know

179
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:57,100
that it's due to HPV 6 and 11.

180
00:06:57,240 --> 00:06:57,900
That's high yield.

181
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:00,540
Laryngeal papilloma HPV 6 and 11.

182
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,660
Of course, because it's HPV,

183
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:03,840
it's going to be associated

184
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:04,980
with coelocytic change

185
00:07:04,980 --> 00:07:06,400
where you're to see it on biopsy.

186
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:08,040
Very high yield to know

187
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:11,180
that laryngeal papilloma is single in adults.

188
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:13,180
It's usually one single lesion in adults,

189
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:15,040
but that in children, it's multiple.

190
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:17,180
And of course, because you have disruption

191
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:18,080
to the vocal cord,

192
00:07:18,380 --> 00:07:20,140
it's going to present with hoarseness.

193
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:23,240
Laryngeal carcinoma is a squamous cell carcinoma

194
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:25,580
that arises from the lining of the vocal cord.

195
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:28,400
Recall that the vocal cord is actually lined

196
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:29,540
by squamous epithelium,

197
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:31,720
and so it's not anything special here

198
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:33,200
that laryngeal carcinoma would be

199
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:34,840
a squamous cell carcinoma.

200
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:36,600
You're well aware of the two risk factors,

201
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:37,720
alcohol and tobacco.

202
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:39,460
Those are the two key risk factors

203
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:41,380
for laryngeal carcinoma.

204
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:43,220
Now, it's important to note that rarely

205
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:45,240
you can get laryngeal carcinoma

206
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:47,020
from a laryngeal papilloma.

207
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:48,440
Remember from the prior slide

208
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:51,500
that laryngeal papillomas are due to HPV 6 and 11.

209
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,840
Remember that 6 and 11 are low risk HPVs.

210
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:57,020
Now, remember, we don't call them zero risk.

211
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:58,540
We call them low risk.

212
00:07:58,620 --> 00:08:01,140
There is a risk for cancer, but it's very low.

213
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:03,840
So it is possible to get laryngeal carcinoma

214
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:05,460
from HPV 6 and 11,

215
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:06,720
but again, that would be rare.

216
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:09,120
Of course, because you've got disruption

217
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:11,500
to the vocal cord, you're going to get hoarseness

218
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:13,740
and other signs of advanced disease

219
00:08:13,740 --> 00:08:15,700
would be cough and stridor.

220
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:18,080
And so these are some of the key presenting signs

221
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:19,880
with laryngeal carcinoma.

222
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:22,640
And that concludes this particular section.

